8. Then it creates a new array `val` with `n` elements from the original array (using `mrb_ary_new_from_values`).

9. Then it handles the shifting of the array:

   a. If the array is shared (i.e., `ARY_SHARED_P(a)` is true), it does:
        a->as.heap.ptr += n;
        a->as.heap.len -= n;
      and returns `val`.

   b. Else, if the array length is greater than `ARY_SHIFT_SHARED_MIN` (a constant), it makes the array shared (via `ary_make_shared(mrb, a)`) and then goes to the label `L_SHIFT` (which is the same as the shared case above).

   c. Else (if the array length is <= `ARY_SHIFT_SHARED_MIN`), it does:
        - If the length equals `n`, it sets the array length to 0 (so the array becomes empty).
