Let me look more carefully at the state migration part:

```c
for (i = 0, mp = m; i < num_migrate; i++, mp++) {
    if ((x = xfrm_migrate_state_find(mp, net))) {
        x_cur[nx_cur] = x;
        nx_cur++;
        xc = xfrm_state_migrate(x, mp, encap);
        if (xc) {
            x_new[nx_new] = xc;
            nx_new++;
        } else {
            err = -ENODATA;
            goto restore_state;
        }
    }
}
```

This loop finds states to migrate, stores them in arrays `x_cur` and `x_new`, and checks if migration was successful. If not, it jumps to `restore_state` to clean up.

The function also has proper cleanup in the `restore_state` label:

```c
restore_state:
    if (pol)
        xfrm_pol_put(pol);
    if (nx_cur)
        xfrm_states_put(x_cur, nx_cur);
    if (nx_new)
        xfrm_states_delete(x_new, nx_new);

    return err;
```

This looks like proper resource cleanup.

